{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I just started a new job and everyone else seems so competent while I feel completely lost.", "response": "Starting something new is one of the most vulnerable places to be, and what you're feeling is completely normal. Every single person around you was once the new person who didn't know where the coffee machine was. Give yourself permission to be a beginner for a few weeks — ask questions, take notes, and trust that competence comes with time, not magic."}
{"prompt": "I'm exhausted from taking care of my aging parents and my own life keeps falling apart.", "response": "You are carrying something truly heavy, and I want you to know that your exhaustion is valid and deserved. Caregiving is invisible labor that drains people slowly, and you're not failing — you're stretching yourself across roles that would overwhelm anyone. It's okay to put down a corner of this weight for just an afternoon. Would it help to look into local caregiver support groups, or even just a home health aide for a few hours a week?"}
{"prompt": "I keep comparing myself to my friends on social media and feeling like my life is falling behind.", "response": "Social media is a highlight reel set to the soundtrack of everyone else's best moments — it's not real life, and it's never a fair comparison. You're seeing their Chapter 20 while you're still writing yours, and both of you have rough drafts you're hiding. Try muting accounts that make you feel small, and give yourself permission to measure progress against where you were a year ago, not against someone else's curated version of today."}
{"prompt": "I tried to apologize to my partner but they said it wasn't enough and I don't know what else to do.", "response": "That's a really painful spot to be in — wanting to make things right but feeling stuck. Sometimes an apology doesn't land because the other person still needs to feel heard before they can accept it. You might try saying something like, \"I hear you that this mattered more than I realized, and I want to understand how it made you feel.\" Let them talk, even if it's hard to hear. It's okay if it takes more than one conversation; showing up patiently is its own kind of apology."}
{"prompt": "I'm 40 and suddenly realizing I don't know what I want from life, and I feel like I've wasted my 20s and 30s.", "response": "Forty is not too late — it's actually a wonderful time for reckoning, because you've had decades of data about what doesn't work for you, and that's valuable information, not wasted time. Some of the best life changes happen in midlife because people finally have the self-awareness to make choices that actually fit. Be kind to your younger self who was just doing their best with what they knew. Now you get to choose something more honest."}
